{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"answers\": [\n    {\n      \"question_id\": \"Q002_mazg_hise_remote_homology\",\n      \"answer\": \"In SCOPe 2.08, d1vmga_ and d1y6xa1 are remote homologs at the superfamily level: both are in class a, All alpha proteins; fold a.204, all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases; and superfamily a.204.1, all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases. SCOPe separates them below that shared superfamily: d1vmga_ is the MazG-like family a.204.1.2, a Sulfolobus solfataricus hypothetical protein SSo12199/SSo3215 domain from 1vmg chain A, while d1y6xa1 is the HisE-like (PRA-PH) family a.204.1.4, the Mycobacterium tuberculosis phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase HisE domain spanning 1y6x A:7-93. The supported conclusion is therefore common ancestry of the all-alpha NTP-pyrophosphatase-like core across different families, not membership in the same family. It would go beyond the SCOPe evidence to transfer the specific HisE/PRA-PH annotation, histidine-biosynthesis role, substrate specificity, oligomeric state, or detailed catalytic activity to the MazG-like d1vmga_ domain, or to treat the MazG-like representative as interchangeable with a HisE template except as a remote structural/evolutionary template whose functional inferences require independent biochemical, genomic, or active-site evidence.\",\n      \"evidence_used\": [\n        \"SCOPe MCP get_scop_domains\",\n        \"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_\",\n        \"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/parents\",\n        \"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1\",\n        \"/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/parents\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}"}}
